#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153033} :activity/id #uuid "e1e5c1d4-ae13-5c28-8065-9304d1a284b4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049405 :topic/id #uuid "f1d3d82f-ded9-5313-ae8c-e1f0314a651b" :topic/math-academy-id 2553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153438 :tutorial/content "Two variables, $x$ and $y,$ are said to have a **proportional relationship** if their ratio is constant. That is, for any $(x,y)$ pair, we have\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a fixed number called the **constant of proportionality** or **constant of variation.**\n\nAs a concrete example, let's consider the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $8$ |\n\nDo $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship?\n\nTo determine whether $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship, we first add a new $\\dfrac yx$ column:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $\\dfrac{y}{x}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| $2$ | $4$ |  |\n| $4$ | $8$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\dfrac yx$ for each row:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $\\dfrac{y}{x}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2\\div 1 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $4\\div 2 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ |\n| $4$ | $8$ | $8\\div 4 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ |\n\nSince all the entries in the third column have the same value, this table does indeed represent a proportional relationship. In this case, the constant of proportionality $k = {\\color{blue}{2}}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "935405f7-2fd3-5fb6-a9ef-e3d74aa721b6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8207 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8723b6ec-51b3-591f-8a78-8d66a882e2c0" :step/math-academy-id 186936 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153034}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152142 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151827 :question/id #uuid "5676578d-255c-51e8-a64f-60e273dabb90" :question/math-academy-id "e-13125"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f15be3c-52df-59c0-82a7-d48736082795" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050138}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497409}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Constant of Proportionality"} :step/id #uuid "86063632-706f-5896-a287-131371bbfac1" :step/math-academy-id 186937 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153035}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152143 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151828 :question/id #uuid "6bbbe009-2488-5442-83e9-98606ca5e78b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13126"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19a2e0a-2271-567a-bd59-12ccbac62ba5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516823}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Table Represents a Proportional Relationship"} :step/id #uuid "174d042b-40fb-57f2-912b-20fe1548d308" :step/math-academy-id 186940 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153036}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153439 :tutorial/content "When the variables $x$ and $y$ form a proportional relationship, the ratio of any $(x,y)$ pair is constant:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac y x = k\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can rewrite this equation above as follows:\n\n$$\ny = kx\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d635aed4-07fa-5ffc-a25c-f9d760dbed7f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8208 :tutorial/title "Finding the Equation of a Proportional Relationship"} :step/id #uuid "387c9c18-edb6-507f-8ae3-d5a4d7a375b6" :step/math-academy-id 186944 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152144 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151829 :question/id #uuid "1c57db95-1fdb-5ea4-894e-0f137b3ef795" :question/math-academy-id "e-13127"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48d2a1f5-e39d-523c-81ed-09426a8045f0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516806}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Proportional Relationship Equation"} :step/id #uuid "4a43a0a6-a976-52ad-b7ac-89aca2144fe1" :step/math-academy-id 186945 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152145 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151830 :question/id #uuid "198d2eae-92a5-57bd-a165-eae20c9a7078" :question/math-academy-id "e-13128"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea5c8e3-3a8e-58a6-b289-84137c2b60cf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050599}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516804}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Data From Proportional Relationships"} :step/id #uuid "faaa86eb-acf9-5373-90c9-af1a462a10aa" :step/math-academy-id 186948}] :activity/title "Understanding Proportional Relationships Using Tables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152311}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516946} :activity/id #uuid "b9af1528-c971-5d9f-8cf2-cea9e5cf8f60" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049405 :topic/id #uuid "f1d3d82f-ded9-5313-ae8c-e1f0314a651b" :topic/math-academy-id 2553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516805 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1232a6a-6ac1-5753-8af1-b9f96be23ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516586} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3a49df8-e648-5d4f-a907-41aa30c55026" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "57a36e7d-b2ab-5ad0-824e-786da584fec4" :question/math-academy-id "q-235791" :question/problem "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $4$ |\n| $16$ | $8$ |\n\nWhat is the equation for the proportional relationship between $x$ and $y$ shown in the table above? Write the answer in terms of the variable $x.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.  \n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ from the table. Here, let's choose the point $(4,2),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2}{4}=\\frac{2÷2}{4÷2}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "42461d35-08fb-56c6-9fa4-431519b12b19"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516946 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0564245c-f5f4-589b-8d5d-c14e4f66edf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{9}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516122} {:answer/id #uuid "1f01cada-e696-5440-a452-4dfbc40584e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{9}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516170} {:answer/id #uuid "289987e7-e2fa-586f-ab12-b1c8fd3c8a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516196} {:answer/id #uuid "843a9341-c217-52c8-952f-f6b0311cc198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516392} {:answer/id #uuid "ee12a623-270c-58d3-80e2-c24e15cbb89a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516606}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516170} :answer-field/id #uuid "90d462db-d663-5b8d-adc9-1cba4b27d754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5aecb393-4227-5488-8434-7689270eefae" :question/math-academy-id "q-68162" :question/problem "Mel's reading a book. There is a proportional relationship between the time $x$ (in minutes) he spends reading and the number of pages $y$ he finishes reading in that time, as shown in the table below: \n\n| Minutes $(x)$ | Pages $(y)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $9$ | $2$ |\n| $18$ | $4$ |\n| $27$ | $6$ |\n\n \n\nWhat is the equation for the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nEvery proportional relationship can be written in the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(9,2),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2}{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{9}x.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a75ac38d-9894-5517-925e-1c5ee926e46f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516978}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516956 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a67e3c9-cbfe-5a48-b6f4-5046d4d76fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516231} {:answer/id #uuid "8312f0f2-c7da-59d2-9b37-57ceb54c1bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516390} {:answer/id #uuid "acba474e-5ca1-5d31-b1a2-f15f2bf31c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516472} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c7f4e5-0d96-536a-b8ab-f765a02f6dff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516589} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5a9ba6-bc9d-5bed-8dbf-3d1b8a8ee92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516589} :answer-field/id #uuid "8421ba5e-d5b7-582e-af56-dbda86d95072" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "31a2e495-8261-514d-8b98-2ab9afe59dfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-169454" :question/problem "Thomas is doing a reading test. The table below shows the relationship between the time $m$ (in minutes) he spends reading and the number of words $w$ he reads.\n\n| Minutes ($m$) | Words ($w$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $70$ |\n| $3.5$ | $245$ |\n| $5$ | $350$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. There is a proportional relationship between $m$ and $w.$\n\nII. There is **not** a proportional relationship between $m$ and $w.$\n\nIII. The constant of proportionality is $35.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's add a new column to our table and compute the ratio of pages per minute for each row.\n\n| Minutes ($m$) | Words ($w$) | Words / Minutes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $70$ | $70$ |\n| $3.5$ | $245$ | $70$ |\n| $5$ | $350$ | $70$ |\n\nThe ratio of words per minute is the same for each row. Therefore, there is a proportional relationship between $m$ and $w,$ and the constant of proportionality is $70.$ \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ba496d7d-c52b-54ec-b734-cc7dd408eb70" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516898}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516823 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51c01f40-d826-53b9-b009-4f85d7aeb79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516280} {:answer/id #uuid "73959195-1a2c-5e4e-a211-783c840bd671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516368} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc2a25f-f66c-56db-825c-fddb05c96f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516543} {:answer/id #uuid "f0699793-6b14-586a-b1a0-309893229379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516608} {:answer/id #uuid "f46b58e5-b476-5250-8911-406159d28052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516619}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516608} :answer-field/id #uuid "33a62cfc-d3c8-5a9e-b734-9abe10311696" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8833a1c2-9810-5a73-b0e7-d02483cf286f" :question/math-academy-id "q-91935" :question/problem "The table below shows the relationship between the number of cars $c$ passing through a particular avenue in $m$ minutes.\n\n| Minutes ($m$) | Cars ($c$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $15$ |\n| $5$ | $25$ |\n| $7$ | $42$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. There is a proportional relationship between $c$ and $m.$\n\nII. There is **not** a proportional relationship between $c$ and $m.$\n\nIII. The constant of proportionality is $11.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's add a new column to our table and compute the ratio of cars per minute for each row.\n\n| Minutes ($m$) | Cars ($c$) | Cars / Minutes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $15$ | $5$ |\n| $5$ | $25$ | $5$ |\n| $7$ | $42$ | $6$ |\n\nThe ratio of cars per minute is not the same for each row. Therefore, there is *not* a proportional relationship between $c$ and $m.$ Consequently, there is no constant of proportionality.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "d1ce1c8a-682c-56a4-b521-3b9b64613a33" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516956}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e9f9f90-1b7a-543c-bd92-a9ee80aa9d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516210} {:answer/id #uuid "437245bb-a588-574f-8c55-b43d00c1311f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516247} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6df8a1-53e3-5f66-8429-4d2fc8d47f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516379} {:answer/id #uuid "8b1890f0-d30e-5a78-a5c3-ff7e78af040d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516401} {:answer/id #uuid "fd496368-49d5-52ef-8b1f-0dafb6172a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516379} :answer-field/id #uuid "7436c522-5721-59b2-8336-da5e30ce4c4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "55df63d6-5603-5ee5-af2f-aa88d2ca3628" :question/math-academy-id "q-68279" :question/problem "There is a proportional relationship between a distance in miles $(m)$ and the same distance in yards $(y)$:\n\n| Miles ($m$) | Yards ($y$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1\\,760$ |\n| $2$ | $3\\,520$ |\n| $3$ | $5\\,280$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The unit rate is $1\\,760$ yards per mile.\n\nII. There are $3\\,760$ yards in $2$ miles.\n\nIII. There are $480$ yards in a quarter of a mile." :question/worked-solution "We're told that $m$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(m,y)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(1,1\\,760),$  which corresponds to the first row of the table. \n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{m}=\\frac{1\\,760}{1}=1\\,760\n$$\n\nThe units of $k$ are \"yards per mile\" since we divided the distance in yard $y$  by the distance in miles $m.$ \n\nTherefore, the relationship between $y$ and $m$ is \n\n$$\ny=1\\,760m.\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, with that in mind, let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. We found above that $k=1\\,760.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The second row of the table corresponds to the point $(2,3\\,520)$ which means that there are $3\\,520$ yards in $2$ miles, not $3\\,760.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $m=\\frac{1}{4}$ into the relationship $y=1\\,760m,$ we get \n\n$$\ny=1\\,760\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=1\\,760÷4=440\n$$\n\nwhich means that there are $440$ yards in a quarter of a mile, not $480.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :step/id #uuid "ea35f357-0efa-512a-a455-3e8c57790590" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516970}}] :activity/title "Understanding Proportional Relationships Using Tables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516106}]}
